WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.890
You start your Spring Boot app, and the logs start scrolling.

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.510
Beans, pools, migrations, endpoints…

3
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.740
But now, how do you know what's actually running in your app?

4
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.210
BootUI answers that, with just one dependency.

5
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:21.210
It's a developer tool that embeds a console right inside your app, for Spring Boot,

6
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.800
with MVC or WebFlux, and for Quarkus.

7
00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.340
It's local-only, it masks your secrets, and it's disabled in production.

8
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.060
Open /bootui in your browser.

9
00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.330
The Scorecard shows your app at a glance:

10
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.390
health, findings, and one known-findings score across every advisor.

11
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.110
Start with the advisors.

12
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.150
BootUI doesn't just show you data:

13
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.790
it tells you what's wrong.

14
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.610
Ten advisors review your architecture, REST API, Spring setup, database,

15
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.280
Hibernate mappings, memory, security and dependencies.

16
00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:02.780
They run only when you ask, and rank every finding by severity, with a concrete fix.

17
00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:07.520
On the sample app, Hibernate flags HIB-FETCH-001:

18
00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:12.290
SampleOrder.customer is fetched eagerly, so every order drags its customer along.

19
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.570
Architecture runs curated ArchUnit rules against your own classes,

20
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.670
and Database inspects your schema and indexes.

21
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.010
Then open Live Activity:

22
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.190
it shows everything your app is doing, as it happens.

23
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.590
Live Flow draws your application map:

24
00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.670
incoming HTTP traffic, the database and the caches,

25
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.510
animated as each request flows through.

26
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.730
Below, every HTTP request is grouped with what it triggered.

27
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.640
This request expands to the four Hibernate sessions it opened.

28
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.030
That one, to the exception it threw.

29
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.580
Click Profile, and you get the full story of a request, correlated exactly,

30
00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.390
with or without tracing.

31
00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:57.060
Then go deeper into the runtime.

32
00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.660
JVM Tuning reviews your JVM arguments,

33
00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.860
and turns a memory budget into ready-to-paste JVM options,

34
00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.900
for a dedicated host or for Kubernetes.

35
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.440
Live Memory shows heap, non-heap and every memory pool, as the JVM runs.

36
00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:16.520
The Spring Security panel lists your filter chains,

37
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.840
and explains which rules protect each endpoint.

38
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.510
SQL Trace captures the statements your app runs, ranks them by time,

39
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.820
and flags N+1 queries.

40
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.350
The PostgreSQL panel reads what the database itself reports:

41
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.140
cache hit ratio, sessions, the most expensive statements,

42
00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.000
index usage and autovacuum health.

43
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.280
And Exceptions groups repeated failures into one entry,

44
00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.970
with the full stack trace and a triage status.

45
00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.060
And that's just the start:

46
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:50.100
health, metrics, threads, beans, configuration, mappings, caches, traces, logs.

47
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.790
BootUI ships more than sixty panels.

48
00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.620
New in version two:

49
00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.360
Runtime Insights.

50
00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:02.280
It connects what a single run did, the requests, the SQL, the transactions,

51
00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:07.220
and flags what no one panel shows, like a write inside a GET request.

52
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.640
Correlation is exact, with or without tracing.

53
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.020
For deeper evidence, attach the optional BootUI Java agent.

54
00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.100
It brings deep visibility into your code's performance,

55
00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:22.060
and unveils issues that are impossible to spot otherwise.

56
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.740
Open the network panel:

57
00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.640
a call to an outside host that no other panel captured.

58
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.450
Still local, and metadata only.

59
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.010
Now bring in your AI agent.

60
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.730
Turn on the local MCP server, or install the BootUI agent skill,

61
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:44.410
and GitHub Copilot, or any MCP client, can call the same ninety-two tools.

62
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.640
Ask your agent to fix the top Hibernate finding.

63
00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:53.610
It runs hibernate_scan, reads the remediation, and switches the association to LAZY.

64
00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.290
Then it scans again:

65
00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.310
four violations become three, verified against the running app,

66
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.290
not guessed from the source.

67
00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:05.620
And when you'd rather stay in your terminal,

68
00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:10.550
the same diagnostics are one command away with the bootui CLI:

69
00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:14.430
exact JSON, ready for scripts and CI.

70
00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.840
One dependency.

71
00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:21.790
One running application, finally understandable.

72
00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.550
BootUI is free and open source, and contributions are welcome.

73
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:31.830
Find it on GitHub, at jdubois/boot-ui, and give it a star!

